---
import StudyHeader from '../../../components/StudyHeader.astro';
import StudyEvidence from '../../../components/StudyEvidence.astro';
import { studies } from '../../../data/studies';
import Site from '../../../layouts/Site.astro';
import MobilityStudyBrief from '../../../components/MobilityStudyBrief.astro';
import MobilityExplorer from '../../../components/MobilityExplorer';
import mobility from '../../../data/mobility-index.json';
import {mobilityAll,mobilityLabels,mobilityPolicies} from '../../../lib/mobility';
import '../../../styles/mobility.css';
const results=mobility.days.map(day=>mobilityAll(mobility.stations,day,200,20));
const average=(p:keyof typeof mobilityLabels)=>results.reduce((s,r)=>s+r[p].residual,0)/results.length;
const delta=average('optimized')-average('no_action');
const worse=results.filter(r=>r.optimized.residual>r.no_action.residual).length;
const comparison=mobility.comparisons.find(r=>r.budget===200&&r.touch_limit===20&&r.baseline==='greedy')!;
const noActionComparison=mobility.comparisons.find(r=>r.budget===200&&r.touch_limit===20&&r.baseline==='no_action')!;
const forecastRows=[1,mobility.meta.selected_history].map(window=>{const rows=mobility.forecast_diagnostics.filter(r=>r.variant==='primary_24h'&&r.window===window);return {window,mae:rows.reduce((s,r)=>s+r.error_num,0)/(60*rows.reduce((s,r)=>s+r.station_count,0))};});
const greedyTies=results.filter(r=>r.optimized.forecast_residual_num===r.greedy.forecast_residual_num).length;
const fmt=(v:number)=>new Intl.NumberFormat('en-GB',{maximumFractionDigits:1}).format(v);
---
<Site title="Where should rebalancing effort go? — cycle-hire allocation" description="Allocate a limited number of bicycle moves across London stations. Explore constrained rebalancing, forecast uncertainty and held-out flow-balance results using TfL Open Data.">
 <StudyHeader study={studies.mobility} />
 <MobilityStudyBrief />
 <section class="tint mobility-explorer" id="mobility-explorer"><div class="shell"><div class="explorer-intro"><h2>Put a limited move budget to work</h2><p>Choose a morning and two effort limits. The model chooses the transfers. Compare its forecast-based plan with the morning that actually occurred.</p><p><a href="#mobility-model">Review the problem and assumptions ↑</a></p></div><MobilityExplorer index={mobility} client:load /><noscript><p>The default evaluated scenario is shown. Enable JavaScript to change dates, budgets or protection, or inspect the Colab notebook below.</p></noscript><p class="source-credit">Powered by <a href="https://tfl.gov.uk/info-for/open-data-users/our-open-data">TfL Open Data</a>. Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database rights 2016. Geomni UK Map data © and database rights [2019]. Independent model: Abdulaziz Aldoseri. No TfL affiliation or endorsement.</p></div></section>
 <section class="section shell study-prose mobility-technical" id="findings"><p class="eyebrow">What the evidence supports</p><h2>Solving the forecast <span class="serif">is only half the question.</span></h2><p>At the reference setting of 200 bike moves and 20 stations, the exact allocation leaves {fmt(Math.abs(delta))} {delta<0?'fewer':'more'} residual flow units per morning than no relocation on average. It worsens the score on {worse} of the 21 test mornings. These are hypothetical residual-flow comparisons, not measured changes to rider service.</p><p>The greedy rule reaches the same forecast objective as the exact allocation on {greedyTies} of those 21 mornings. On observed outcomes, the exact allocation’s average advantage over greedy is only {fmt(Math.abs(comparison.mean_difference))} flow units: {comparison.better} mornings improve and {comparison.tied} tie. At 400 bikes and 40 stations, the exact plan is worse than greedy on five mornings. A better forecast objective does not guarantee a better later outcome. Use the period comparison to explore those cases.</p><p>The forecast uses a {mobility.meta.selected_history}-week same-weekday horizon, selected on the earlier validation period from 1, 2, 4 and 6 weeks. The first test morning, 2 March, and default budgets were fixed before evaluation.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Full-period policy comparison"><table><caption>All 21 test mornings · 200-bike budget · 20-station limit · no protection</caption><thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Mean observed residual</th><th>Mean bikes moved</th><th>Mean stations changed</th></tr></thead><tbody>{mobilityPolicies.map(p=><tr><th scope="row">{mobilityLabels[p]}</th><td>{fmt(average(p))}</td><td>{fmt(results.reduce((s,r)=>s+r[p].moves,0)/results.length)}</td><td>{fmt(results.reduce((s,r)=>s+r[p].touched,0)/results.length)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p>The perfect-information reference is an optimistic comparison under the same abstract constraints. It knows the observed flow in advance. Historical inventories and physical dispatch feasibility remain unknown even for that reference.</p><p class="scope-note">The descriptive seven-day block-resampling interval for the mean exact-minus-greedy residual is {fmt(comparison.interval![0])} to {fmt(comparison.interval![1])}; against no relocation it is {fmt(noActionComparison.interval![0])} to {fmt(noActionComparison.interval![1])}. These intervals resample the same short winter sequence. They do not establish performance in another season or network.</p></section>
 <section class="section tint" id="method"><div class="shell study-prose mobility-technical"><p class="eyebrow">Evaluation &amp; preparation</p><h2>Keep every assumption traceable.</h2><div class="method-grid"><article><h3>Fix the cohort first</h3><p>Six training weeks admit 798 stations with activity in at least four weeks. Five other ordinary stations stay in the full observed network with zero intervention. One first appears during the test period.</p></article><article><h3>Keep the clock honest</h3><p>Arrivals and departures use their own event times in 06:00–10:00. Prior-date starts are included. Source times have no explicit offset; London winter local time is an assumption, and the test ends before the clock change.</p></article><article><h3>Retain the awkward cases</h3><p>The primary scope includes CLASSIC journeys of at most 24 hours. Separate 12-hour, 48-hour and unrestricted-duration diagnostics keep the same station cohort. All 21 test dates and 35 effort settings are available in the results.</p></article></div>
 <details class="data-details"><summary>What is filtered—and what remains uncertain?</summary><p>The six unchanged source files contain 1,897,417 unique journeys. After the CLASSIC-bike, 24-hour-duration and whole-journey endpoint screens, 1,528,709 journeys remain across the source dates. A journey is excluded if either terminal label contains “workshop” or “_OLD”; terminal IDs are never merged. The full test network records 104,491 morning departures and 99,512 arrivals across 803 ordinary terminal IDs.</p><p>Days before a station’s first eligible primary event are unavailable history. Later days with no records contribute zero recorded activity; this does not prove station availability or zero demand. Means use only earlier same-weekday dates, at least seven days back, with sample counts shown for each station. Rolling updates follow a fixed rule.</p><p>The unrestricted-duration diagnostic is left-censored by the January-1 start cohort and includes knowledge of later completions. It is not a full-population or as-issued evaluation. Alternate targets must be compared within their own definitions; a lower residual after filtering is not itself better performance.</p><p>The publication files were modified in June, after the January–March events. This retrospective study assumes operator access to prior completed observations; it does not demonstrate that the public feed was available at each historical cutoff.</p></details>
 <details class="data-details"><summary>Forecast accuracy and duration sensitivity</summary><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Forecast comparison"><table><caption>Admitted stations · all 21 test mornings · net-flow bikes</caption><thead><tr><th>Forecast horizon</th><th>Mean absolute error</th></tr></thead><tbody>{forecastRows.map(r=><tr><th scope="row">{r.window===1?'Same weekday last week':`${r.window} previous same weekdays`}</th><td>{fmt(r.mae)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Duration sensitivity"><table><caption>200 bikes · 20 stations · fixed cohort · exact allocation</caption><thead><tr><th>Journey-duration scope</th><th>Mean observed residual</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">At most 24 hours · primary</th><td>{fmt(average('optimized'))}</td></tr>{mobility.sensitivity_summaries.filter(r=>r.budget===200&&r.touch_limit===20&&r.policy==='optimized').map(r=><tr><th scope="row">{{duration_12h:'At most 12 hours',duration_48h:'At most 48 hours',duration_unrestricted:'Unrestricted · left-censored diagnostic'}[r.variant]}</th><td>{fmt(r.mean_residual)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p>Each screen defines a different recorded-flow target. Compare policies within a target; lower residual across differently filtered targets is not a policy improvement. The Colab notebook includes all policies, dates and settings for every variant.</p></details>
 <p class="scope-note">Before dispatch, an operator would also need historical stock and dock availability, within-morning inventory paths, vehicle capacity, travel times and costs. This study supports investigation of effort concentration; it does not claim stockout reductions, cost savings, route feasibility or a deployed service.</p></div></section>
 <StudyEvidence title="Open journeys. Reproducible choices." notebook="mobility">
<Fragment slot="description"><p>Transport for London, Santander Cycle Hire journey data, January–March 2026. Retrieved 14 September 2026. The reproduction package identifies the six source files, their hashes, transformations, frozen protocol, model and complete results. Public data are station/day aggregates; journey and bike identifiers are excluded.</p></Fragment>
<p><a href="https://cycling.data.tfl.gov.uk/">Original journey files</a> · <a href="https://tfl.gov.uk/corporate/terms-and-conditions/transport-data-service">TfL data terms</a></p><p class="source-credit">Powered by TfL Open Data. Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database rights 2016. Geomni UK Map data © and database rights [2019]. Changes: CLASSIC and terminal screening, duration variants, event-time aggregation and training-defined intervention cohort. No endorsement is implied.</p>
</StudyEvidence>
</Site>
